<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grésivaudan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Grésivaudan is the wide valley of the Isère upstream from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenoble">Grenoble</a>, in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, running north-east between the Belledonne and Chartreuse mountains towards Savoy. It was one of the first lands gathered by the counts of Albon, the future Dauphins, in the 11th century, and later a border zone with Savoy. Its fertile floor and water power supported farming and, from the 19th century, industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gresivaudan.jpg" alt="Grésivaudan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Cevenol2 at French Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 2.0 fr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Croix_de_Belledonne_082004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
